"TTM Technologies (TTMI) Q3 2026 Earnings Call Transcript & Audio (https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/ttmi/transcripts/725340-q3-2026/)\nciteturn22view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn21view0\",\"pattern\":\"capital\"}); Total lines: 256\nL127: Consequently, we expect the potential combined offerings of Epiq Solutions and Ophir Optics can advance a common strategy moving up the defense value chain and bringing together complementary software-defined radios and infrared capabilities. Both are keys to expanding our role in mission-critical sensing modalities such as surveillance, reconnaissance, communications, and electronic warfare.\nL128: In industrial, this proposed acquisition provides laser optical subsystems used for shaping laser beams in industrial cutting, welding, and PCB drilling machines. We are very excited for the opportunities ahead. From a financial perspective, the proposed acquisition of Ophir Optics is projected to incrementally benefit our financial model by adding more high-margin, long-cycle business to our mix. Let me go through the financial highlights with you.\nL129: We are acquiring Ophir Optics business for an all-cash purchase of $650 million as a carve-out from MKS. The transaction is expected to be funded through a combination of cash on hand and borrowings under TTM's existing revolving credit facility. At this time, we believe it's important to convey to all our shareholders that we do not have any current incremental needs or plans to come to the financial markets to access capital.\nL130: On timing, we expect to close this in the second quarter of 2027, subject to regulatory approvals and other customary closing conditions. Ophir Optics experiencing strong revenue growth and margin expansion, and the transaction is expected to be immediately accretive to our adjusted EBITDA margins and our non-GAAP diluted EPS. The transaction multiple is estimated to be roughly 10x the expected Ophir Optics full year 2027 adjusted EBITDA without synergies.\nL131: TTM's net leverage at closing is expected to be less than 2x adjusted last 12 months EBITDA, and therefore, within our targeted leverage range. I'll now move on to an overview of our business highlights from the quarter. We'll follow up with a summary on our Q3 2026 financial performance, our Q4 and full year 2026 guidance, and our initial preliminary guidance for 2027.\nL132: From a demand standpoint, product developments with our partners, bookings and backlogs, and broader signals in both AI and defense remain strong and are expected to provide very healthy growth momentum for the remainder of this year, and as we look in 2027.\nL133: We interact very closely with our customers and strategic partners, and jointly engage in very diligent conversations to plan for the future, make thoughtful investments, and take a disciplined approach to vet our outlook, which we believe provides us with a lot of optimism as will be evidenced in the guidance that Dan will share shortly. We delivered an excellent third quarter of 2026, and as always, I would like to thank our employees for delivering these results.\nL134: We achieved sales of $1.2 billion and non-GAAP EPS of $1.46 per diluted share, both above our guidance and both all-time quarterly highs. Sales for consolidated TTM grew 60% year-on-year, reflecting demand strength in our Data Center & Networking end markets driven by AI, while our medical, industrial, and instrumentation sales almost doubled from a year ago. The Aerospace & Defense end markets experienced very strong double-digit growth.\nL168: But again, currently about 30% of the Data Center & Networking business going in the next year to about 50%. So that technology is working really well. Great. That's helpful. Then just to be crystal clear on all the M&A you've done and how you're planning to finance it, can you sort of talk about where this leaves you? What's the potential for more M&A once you close all these deals? Just big picture thoughts on balance sheet, since you've been pretty active in the acquisition market the last few months.\nL169: Thanks. Yeah, Steve, this is Dan. I'll take that. Thanks for the question. Yeah, I think this will be the end of the big stuff for a while. There may be a few little things that we've announced, a few that we need to close. One of those kind of medium-sized, the other smaller. So we'll focus on strengthening our balance sheet, getting our leverage back down now. We've got a lot of strong cash flow coming in next year.\nL170: We'll have positive free cash flow to continue to fund our capital expenditures as well as start paying down that debt. But in our Q4 guide, the difference between where we were last quarter and this quarter, we do have two of the businesses that have closed or are expected to close in this period or close right at the end of Q3 into Q4, will impact positively on our EBITDA. But we did raise significant debt for the purchase of Epiq, as well as to finance the Ophir project that we just announced.\nL171: So that has an increase of interest expense for about $20 million a quarter. So that drags on our EPS. So you look at the change in EPS guidance from last quarter to where we are now, up from the transactions, but then offset by the debt. Great. That's really helpful. Thank you. Thank you. One moment for our next question. That will come from the line of Will Stein with Truist. Your line is open. Thanks for taking my questions.\nL172: First, I want to recognize and say congratulations on the great results overall. But I want to also recognize that the Data Center & Networking end market came about 10% below your prior guidance in Q3. So I'm hoping you can help us understand what happened there. Was it supply or material availability or yield or something else? Hi, Will. Good morning. None of these things. These things basically have to do with project timing on the customer side.\nL173: Again, these large customers, these big customers we have, they have multiple programs, and there are always fluctuations in these programs. Some of them move to the left, some of them move a bit to the right. Again, there are no technical issues. Ramp is going well. Yield numbers are where they should be. Again, they have always multiple suppliers. There's always a bit of, let's say, everybody has to be in that same window technology-wise. So that's sometimes hurting us also a bit.\nL174: But again, 2 out of these 3 programs we initially had are doing really well. The third one is ramping up now. That will be in ramp-up in the second half of Q4, fully in production in Q1. So that's going smooth. It's like I said before there are more N plus M programs coming. But again, there will always be a fluctuation, and we always try to be as accurate as possible in our guidance. But again, there are no major issues there. Appreciate that.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTTM Technologies (TTMI) Q3 2026 Earnings Call Transcript & Audio (https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/ttmi/transcripts/725340-q3-2026/)\nciteturn22view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn21view0\",\"pattern\":\"2027\"}); Total lines: 256\nL112: We already have visibility into roughly 65% of our projected A&D revenue for 2027. Our medical, industrial, and instrumentation and automotive end markets also outperformed our expectation year to date. With all our business performing well in 2026 to date, we are increasing our full year 2026 revenue guidance from $4.47 billion to $4.49 billion.\nL113: Since our last quarterly earnings call, we have had many exciting developments here at TTM Technologies, and we continue to execute our long-term business strategy focused on projected value creation for our customers and shareholders.\nL127: Consequently, we expect the potential combined offerings of Epiq Solutions and Ophir Optics can advance a common strategy moving up the defense value chain and bringing together complementary software-defined radios and infrared capabilities. Both are keys to expanding our role in mission-critical sensing modalities such as surveillance, reconnaissance, communications, and electronic warfare.\nL128: In industrial, this proposed acquisition provides laser optical subsystems used for shaping laser beams in industrial cutting, welding, and PCB drilling machines. We are very excited for the opportunities ahead. From a financial perspective, the proposed acquisition of Ophir Optics is projected to incrementally benefit our financial model by adding more high-margin, long-cycle business to our mix. Let me go through the financial highlights with you.\nL129: We are acquiring Ophir Optics business for an all-cash purchase of $650 million as a carve-out from MKS. The transaction is expected to be funded through a combination of cash on hand and borrowings under TTM's existing revolving credit facility. At this time, we believe it's important to convey to all our shareholders that we do not have any current incremental needs or plans to come to the financial markets to access capital.\nL130: On timing, we expect to close this in the second quarter of 2027, subject to regulatory approvals and other customary closing conditions. Ophir Optics experiencing strong revenue growth and margin expansion, and the transaction is expected to be immediately accretive to our adjusted EBITDA margins and our non-GAAP diluted EPS. The transaction multiple is estimated to be roughly 10x the expected Ophir Optics full year 2027 adjusted EBITDA without synergies.\nL131: TTM's net leverage at closing is expected to be less than 2x adjusted last 12 months EBITDA, and therefore, within our targeted leverage range. I'll now move on to an overview of our business highlights from the quarter. We'll follow up with a summary on our Q3 2026 financial performance, our Q4 and full year 2026 guidance, and our initial preliminary guidance for 2027.\nL132: From a demand standpoint, product developments with our partners, bookings and backlogs, and broader signals in both AI and defense remain strong and are expected to provide very healthy growth momentum for the remainder of this year, and as we look in 2027.\nL133: We interact very closely with our customers and strategic partners, and jointly engage in very diligent conversations to plan for the future, make thoughtful investments, and take a disciplined approach to vet our outlook, which we believe provides us with a lot of optimism as will be evidenced in the guidance that Dan will share shortly. We delivered an excellent third quarter of 2026, and as always, I would like to thank our employees for delivering these results.\nL134: We achieved sales of $1.2 billion and non-GAAP EPS of $1.46 per diluted share, both above our guidance and both all-time quarterly highs. Sales for consolidated TTM grew 60% year-on-year, reflecting demand strength in our Data Center & Networking end markets driven by AI, while our medical, industrial, and instrumentation sales almost doubled from a year ago. The Aerospace & Defense end markets experienced very strong double-digit growth.\nL135: The company's adjusted EBITDA margin was 20.1% in the third quarter of 2026, up an impressive 400 basis points year-over-year and over 350 basis points sequentially, reflecting positive mix impacts and operational excellence. Non-GAAP EPS of $1.46 per diluted share was 118% improvement year-on-year. The Aerospace & Defense end market represented 34% of third quarter 2026 sales.\nL136: Sales in the Aerospace & Defense market grew 21% year-on-year in the third quarter, with the vast majority of our facilities performing at strong levels. The sales growth in the defense market continues to be the result of positive tailwinds in defense budgets, our strong strategic program alignment, and key bookings for new and ongoing programs. Our ability to support sustained long-term growth is also very encouraging and further enabled by our recent strategic acquisitions.\nL137: Our focus on product innovation is tracking well against our internal roadmaps for many new capabilities, including unique advancements in printed circuit board technologies for materials that enable high frequency in support of all our applications.\nL138: During the third quarter of 2026, our A&D team's effective execution continued in securing another strong bookings quarter, capturing business related to the Surface Ship Surveillance, SPY-6 radar, the APS-143 maritime surveillance radar, Golden Dome, and a number of protected priority restricted programs. A&D's book-to-bill ratio was roughly 1.6 for the quarter, which led to total program backlog of $2 billion, a record level up from just under $1.5 billion a year ago.\nL139: Within our backlog, we are excited to report that we have strong momentum securing new business from new primes, as well as restricted programs. Restricted programs are incredibly sticky and very difficult for competitors to try and displace. For the fourth quarter of 2026, we expect this end market to represent approximately 31% of our total sales and continue to deliver both year-on-year and sequential growth.\nL156: Free cash flow in the third quarter of 2026 was $74.8 million, as compared to free cash flow of $42.6 million in the third quarter of last year. Now we will provide our financial guidance for the fourth quarter of 2026, an update to our outlook for the full year of 2026, and an initial preliminary expectation for full year 2027. We project net sales for the fourth quarter of 2026 to be in the range of $1.42 billion-$1.44 billion, and non-GAAP earnings to be in the range of $1.77-$1.83 per diluted share.\nL157: These projected results include the contributions of recently closed transactions and associated financing. However, they exclude any contribution or impact from pending acquisitions. Consequently, we now expect full year 2026 sales of approximately $4.47 billion-$4.49 billion, and non-GAAP earnings in the range of $4.97-$5.03 per diluted share.\nL158: These full year 2026 projections also include the contributions of recently closed transactions and associated financing, but exclude any contribution or impact from pending acquisitions. The fourth quarter 2026 non-GAAP diluted EPS forecast is based on a diluted share count of approximately 107.8 million shares, which includes the dilutive effect of outstanding stock options and other stock awards.\nL159: Our expected full year 2026 non-GAAP EPS forecast is based on a diluted share count of 107.5 million shares, similarly including the effect of outstanding stock options and other stock awards. We expect SG&A expense to be approximately 6.7% of net sales in the fourth quarter, and R&D expenditures to be approximately 1% of net sales.\nL160: We expect interest expense of approximately $33.9 million, interest income of approximately $3.0 million, and realized foreign exchange and other non-operating expenses of approximately $4.7 million. We estimate our effective tax rate will be between 15% and 19%.\nL161: Further, we expect to record depreciation of approximately $39.7 million, amortization of intangibles of approximately $12.1 million, stock-based compensation expense of approximately $18.7 million, and non-cash interest expense of approximately $0.9 million. Looking to the preliminary full year 2027 forecast, we are providing our initial net sales forecast to be in the range of approximately $6.5 billion to $6.75 billion.\nL162: Non-GAAP net income is expected to be in the range of $8.10 to $8.90 per diluted share based on a diluted share count of 108.1 million shares, including the effect of outstanding stock options and other stock awards. Our initial 2027 full year forecast includes contributions from recently closed acquisitions and associated financing costs, while excluding any contribution or impact from pending acquisitions.\nL163: Finally, I'd like to announce that we are scheduled to participate in the Bank of America Leveraged Finance Conference in Boca Raton, Florida, on December 1st, and the UBS TMT Conference in Scottsdale, Arizona, on December 2nd. That concludes our prepared remarks, so I'll turn it back to you, Sheree, for the Q&A session. Thank you. As a reminder, to ask a question, please press *11 on your telephone and wait for your name to be announced. To withdraw your question, press *11 again.\nL164: Due to time restraints, we ask that you please limit yourself to one question and one follow-up question. Please stand by while we compile the Q&A roster. Our first question will come from the line of Steven Fox with Fox Advisors. Your line is open. Hi, good morning. I guess for my first question, I was wondering if we could sort of dig into the N plus M results to date and expectations going forward.\nL165: Can you give us a sense for how much of the quarter was driven by the new product, where you think you are in terms of ramping yields, et cetera, and then how broad-based the product is expected to be used? I'm just trying to understand how dominant this is becoming within your traditional PCB sales. Then I had a follow-up. Yeah, Steve, good morning. Yes, happy to do that. Our N plus M program will be a substantial part of our Data Center & Networking group, as you know. Ramp-ups are going really well.\nL179: Price increases, yes the customers will absorb that. Okay. And then just to follow up related, the higher ASPs for the N plus M, we understand that, but would you say that is higher than corporate average gross margin? I guess related, Yes where do you see gross and EBITDA margins progressing throughout 2027? Yes. These ASPs are growing faster than our cost. That has to do with the complexity of the boards. The complexity is increasing a lot for these boards. Basically, it is all about the value.\nL183: These programs are doing well. Again, the feedback is very, very positive from these larger customers that basically more N plus M programs are coming in. Okay. Then on the $400 million capacity expansion, it sounds like that will be fully operational in 2028. Can you talk about what kind of customer volume visibility you have against that increased commitment? Are customers committing to programs against that spend? Thank you. Yeah. Happy to do that.\nL184: The $400 million is going on top of something we addressed earlier, a $1.2 billion expansion, let's say, over multiple years till 2029. The $400 million, I would say, is more allocated to N plus M. So I would say the $400 million is pure N plus M, rounding numbers here. Yeah? You have to think about our brownfield approach. So basically, what we're very good at, and you see it in China, and I gave several examples during the investor meetings, is that we love brownfields.\nL185: So basically, buildings close to our existing plants, we are going to utilize for additional capacity. We do that very, very fast, and we can follow the market much more accurate. So that's basically all of that. It's a lot of equipment, Ruben, which we are installing there to grow our N plus M. It's like I said, DCN, our Data Center & Networking business, 50% of that will be N plus M. So that's going really well. The technology is really doing what it should do. Great. Thanks, Edwin.\nL186: One moment for our next question. That will come from the line of Jim Ricchiuti with Needham. Your line is open. Hi. Thank you. Good morning. Hopefully, can you hear me okay? Yeah. Good. Have a good day. Great. So, wanted to just ask you about the preliminary 2027 guidance.\nL187: Jim, Edwin, relative to your earlier expectations, the growth that you are expecting now for 2027, how much of that is due to perhaps higher N plus M expectations for data center or possibly how much is due to higher organic growth in the A&D business? Yeah. So, if you look at the starting, Jim, with the numbers, let's say for this year, the $4.4 billion-$4.5 billion, let's say, is a 48% growth year-over-year. This is a number we are going to repeat next year. Yeah. And again, next year we will grow 50%.\nL188: A substantial part of that growth will be N plus M. The interesting thing, Jim, is that all the different end markets are growing. So if you look at our medical industrial instrumentation business, it is growing a lot, and it will continue to grow in next year. Even if you look at our automotive business is growing year-over-year. And of course, our Aerospace & Defense business is growing a lot next year.\nL189: So it is just a matter that Data Center & Networking is growing so fast that it does not look like the others are growing, but I can tell you it is growing over 20%. Yeah. These are big numbers. So yes, we are confident with the numbers we predicted for 2027. Got it. Follow-up question relates to the M&A activity. So we have Epiq, other smaller M&A that you have done and the pending Ophir acquisition.\nL190: If we look out to next year after Ophir closes, how much of the A&D business is going to be non-PCB in nature now? At the moment, if you look at our Aerospace & Defense business, about half of them is what we call up the chain. Our, let's say, sensing and communication activities. If we look at Epiq, and Ophir, by the way, they are very complementary. If you look at Epiq, let's say that is more the processing, and Ophir, we are very happy with that one as well. That is more the optical side.\nL191: If you have the optical side and let's say the processing under control and you are sensor agnostic, you basically can make full sensing systems, which are very consistently used in complementary to our radar activity. That is going well. If you look at the numbers, all of our acquisitions, and, of course Ophir will only add, let's say, the second part of the year because we only close it, let's say, hopefully in Q2, at the end of Q2, so we have only one half year of Ophir.\nL192: Then I would say if you add up all these acquisitions, it is about half a billion in sales of the $6.75, $6.5 for next year. So that is about the ratio. Got it. Thank you. Yeah. Okay. Thank you. One moment for our next question. We do have a follow-up question from Will Stein with Truist. Your line is open. Great. Thanks for taking the follow-up. I wanted to ask about Ophir.\nL193: When we look at the EBITDA multiples cited in your press release and in the seller's press release, one conclusion is that EBITDA is growing very significantly, or you expect it to grow very significantly from 2026 to 2027. What is driving that? Is that backlog that is already in place, or is that stuff that you expect to get design wins after the close? Any color to sort of explain this to me? Yes, Will, that is a very good question. Yes, you gave the answer yourself.\nL194: The thing is, if we look at the bookings for 2027, Ophir is over 85% booked with new things coming in. By the way, they have a tendency that they always beat their forecast, by the way. So, 2027, 85% booked. The 10.3 we addressed as being the multiple EBITDA over the price we pay, is probably even a bit more conservative. I think it was a very, very, very nice deal for us, around 10 times. So we are very happy with that. It is like I said, the synergies are not taking into the business case at all.\nL195: But the synergies will be huge because this is giving us the optical system. We have the processing. We can be sensor-agnostic. That basically means we can make a full optical system. It is a very nice acquisition for us. Then, as a follow-up, you have now given us a preliminary view on 2027. Normally, when you have guided revenue, you have given us some breakdown by end market.\nL196: I wonder if you are willing to do that in this case for 2027, how we should think about each end market, as percentage of revenues. It's a bit early for that build, but of course, we will provide more color. Again, only a few quarters ago, we gave only, let's say, forecast for the next quarter. Now, let's say we are giving a bit more guidance for the full year. You have an idea what our Data Center & Networking business is doing, how that will grow. Our A&D business will do better and better.\nL197: Let's say, looking at the munition side, looking at the Golden Dome side, a lot of radar. So that will do better and better. It's a bit early to give that exact split, but we wanted to give some guidance for 2027, and showing that we are maintaining that growth. Well, I think we've done that in the past in the fourth quarter call, and so we'll strive to give that. We'll be able to give that in the next call. Thank you. Thank you. I'm showing no further questions in the queue at this time.\nL198: I'll turn the call back over to Mr. Edwin Roks for any closing remarks. Yeah, thank you. This has been a good call, so I thank you, operator. Now, let's summarize with a few items. First of all, we are seeing very strong growth. We delivered very strong sales growth in Q3 of about 60% year-on-year, resulting in an all-time high of $1.2 billion for quarterly revenue, driven by increase in our Data Center & Networking, medical, industrial, instrumentation, Aerospace & Defense markets.\nL199: Basically, all the markets helped here. Second, our adjusted EBITDA for the second quarter of 20.1% reflected strong operating improvements and overall performance, leading to another all-time high record in non-GAAP EPS. As a result, $1.46 per diluted share, and expect to continue building on this operating performance in the coming quarters.\nL200: Third, we continue to generate solid cash flows from operations, which is used to our source to invest in our organic operations and to support projected or continued growth. In closing, I said it before, I would like to thank all our employees of TTM, our customers, our suppliers, and our shareholders for continued support, and 